[
{ch:15,q:"What does entropy, S, measure?",choices:["The number of possible microstates, or degree of dispersal/disorder, of a system","The total chemical energy stored within a system","The rate at which a reaction proceeds towards equilibrium","The total mass of a system's reactants and products"],explain:"Entropy, S, is a measure of the number of ways particles and energy can be arranged (accessible microstates) in a system &ndash; its degree of dispersal or disorder."},
{ch:15,q:"What are the correct standard units for entropy, S&deg;?",choices:["J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","kJ K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Entropy values are quoted in joules (not kilojoules) per kelvin per mole: J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Mixing this up with the kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> units of enthalpy is a common source of error."},
{ch:15,q:"Why is confusing the units of &Delta;S (J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) with the units of &Delta;H (kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) a serious error when calculating &Delta;G?",choices:["Because &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S combines both terms, so failing to convert one of them introduces an error of a factor of 1000","Because entropy and enthalpy can never be combined in the same equation under any circumstances","Because &Delta;S is always exactly 1000 times smaller than &Delta;H for any reaction","Because the gas constant R only works with enthalpy values given in kJ"],explain:"Since &Delta;H is normally in kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S in J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, one of the two must be converted before combining them in &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S, or the result will be wrong by a factor of 1000."},
{ch:15,q:"What is the entropy of a perfectly ordered crystal at absolute zero (0 K)?",choices:["Exactly zero","A small positive value, close to zero but never equal to zero","A large negative value","It cannot be defined at 0 K"],explain:"A perfectly ordered crystal at 0 K has only one possible microstate, so its entropy is defined as exactly zero; every other state, at any temperature above 0 K, has some positive entropy."},
{ch:15,q:"Which of the following is true of all absolute entropy values, S&deg;, for real substances above 0 K?",choices:["They are always positive","They are always negative","They can be positive or negative depending on the substance","They are always exactly zero for elements"],explain:"Unlike enthalpy of formation, which is zero for elements and can be positive or negative for compounds, absolute entropy values are always positive for any substance above 0 K."},
{ch:15,q:"Under the same conditions, how do the standard entropies of the three physical states of a given substance compare?",choices:["S&deg;(gas) &gt;&gt; S&deg;(liquid) &gt; S&deg;(solid)","S&deg;(solid) &gt; S&deg;(liquid) &gt; S&deg;(gas)","S&deg;(gas) = S&deg;(liquid) = S&deg;(solid) always","S&deg;(liquid) &gt; S&deg;(gas) &gt; S&deg;(solid)"],explain:"Gas particles have far more accessible positions and a continuous range of kinetic energies compared with a liquid, and a liquid is more disordered than a solid, so S&deg;(gas) &gt;&gt; S&deg;(liquid) &gt; S&deg;(solid)."},
{ch:15,q:"When predicting the entropy change of a chemical reaction, which factor is usually dominant?",choices:["A change in the number of moles of gas","A change in the number of moles of solid","A change in the number of moles of liquid","The overall mass of the reaction mixture"],explain:"A change in the number of moles of gas usually has a far greater effect on entropy than any change in the number of moles of solid or liquid, since gas particles are vastly more dispersed."},
{ch:15,q:"How does dissolving a solid into aqueous solution generally affect the entropy of the system?",choices:["It generally increases entropy, since the solute particles become dispersed throughout the solvent","It generally decreases entropy, since the solute is confined within the solution","It has no effect on entropy under any circumstances","It always decreases entropy for ionic solids but increases it for covalent solids"],explain:"Dissolving a solid or liquid into solution generally increases entropy because the particles become dispersed throughout the solvent rather than confined to a fixed lattice or separate layer."},
{ch:15,q:"How does increasing temperature generally affect the entropy of a substance?",choices:["Entropy increases, as thermal energy becomes spread over more accessible energy levels","Entropy decreases, as particles move faster and become more ordered","Entropy is completely independent of temperature","Entropy only changes with temperature for gases, never for solids or liquids"],explain:"As temperature rises, the available thermal energy becomes spread over an increasing number of accessible energy levels, so entropy increases with temperature for any substance."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does a larger, more structurally complex molecule generally have a higher molar entropy than a smaller, simpler one in the same physical state?",choices:["It has more internal degrees of freedom (more ways to vibrate and rotate), over which energy can be dispersed","It always has a larger molar mass, and entropy depends only on mass","It contains more atoms of oxygen, which always increases entropy","Molecular complexity has no effect on entropy"],explain:"A larger, more complex molecule has more internal degrees of freedom (vibrations, rotations) over which its energy can be spread, giving it a higher molar entropy than a smaller, simpler molecule in the same state."},
{ch:15,q:"Predict the sign of &Delta;S for Br<sub>2</sub>(l) &rarr; Br<sub>2</sub>(g).",choices:["Positive","Negative","Zero","Cannot be predicted without a calculation"],explain:"One mole of liquid becomes one mole of gas, increasing dispersal of matter, so &Delta;S is positive."},
{ch:15,q:"Predict the sign of &Delta;S for 2Cu(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2CuO(s).",choices:["Negative","Positive","Zero","Cannot be predicted without a calculation"],explain:"One mole of gas is consumed and no gas is produced, decreasing the dispersal of matter, so &Delta;S is negative."},
{ch:15,q:"Predict the sign of &Delta;S for H<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HCl(g).",choices:["Approximately zero, since the number of moles of gas is unchanged","Strongly positive, since a new compound is formed","Strongly negative, since a bond is broken","Cannot be predicted at all for any gas-phase reaction"],explain:"There are two moles of gas among the reactants and two moles of gas among the products, so there is no significant change in gaseous dispersal, and &Delta;S is close to zero."},
{ch:15,q:"Predict the sign of &Delta;S for Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; AgBr(s).",choices:["Negative","Positive","Zero","Cannot be predicted without a calculation"],explain:"Two moles of dissolved, dispersed ions combine to form one mole of ordered solid, decreasing dispersal, so &Delta;S is negative."},
{ch:15,q:"Which equation correctly calculates the standard entropy change of a reaction from standard molar entropy values?",choices:["&Delta;S&deg; = &Sigma;S&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants)","&Delta;S&deg; = &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants) &minus; &Sigma;S&deg;(products)","&Delta;S&deg; = &Sigma;S&deg;(products) + &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants)","&Delta;S&deg; = &Sigma;S&deg;(products) &times; &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants)"],explain:"Just as for enthalpy of formation, &Delta;S&deg;(reaction) = &Sigma;S&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants)."},
{ch:15,q:"Why, unlike the calculation of &Delta;H&deg; from &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg; values, is there no need to subtract an 'entropy of formation' when calculating &Delta;S&deg; from S&deg; values?",choices:["Because the entropy of an element in its standard state is not zero, so absolute entropies can be used directly","Because elements never appear as reactants in any entropy calculation","Because &Delta;S&deg; can only ever be calculated using bond enthalpies","Because entropy values do not depend on physical state"],explain:"Unlike &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;, which is zero for an element in its standard state, the absolute entropy S&deg; of an element is not zero, so absolute molar entropies can be used directly in &Delta;S&deg; = &Sigma;S&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants)."},
{ch:15,q:"Using S&deg;(N<sub>2</sub>) = 191, S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>) = 131 and S&deg;(NH<sub>3</sub>) = 193 (all J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Sigma;S&deg;(products) for the Haber process, N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g).",choices:["386 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","193 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","579 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","584 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Sigma;S&deg;(products) = 2 &times; 193 = 386 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"Using S&deg;(N<sub>2</sub>) = 191 and S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>) = 131 (J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants) for the Haber process, N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g).",choices:["584 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","322 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","393 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","191 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Sigma;S&deg;(reactants) = 191 + (3 &times; 131) = 191 + 393 = 584 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"Combining &Sigma;S&deg;(products) = 386 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Sigma;S&deg;(reactants) = 584 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, calculate &Delta;S&deg; for the Haber process, N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g).",choices:["&minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;970 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+970 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;S&deg; = 386 &minus; 584 = &minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, a substantial decrease, since 4 mol of gaseous reactants form only 2 mol of gaseous product."},
{ch:15,q:"Why is it sensible to predict the sign of &Delta;S&deg; before calculating it numerically for the Haber process?",choices:["Because 4 mol of gas becomes 2 mol of gas, so a decrease in entropy is expected, letting you check the calculated value makes sense","Because the sign of &Delta;S&deg; can never actually be predicted in advance for any reaction","Because &Delta;S&deg; is always positive for any reaction involving ammonia","Because predicting the sign replaces the need for any numerical calculation"],explain:"Since the total number of moles of gas decreases from 4 to 2 in the Haber process, a decrease in entropy (negative &Delta;S&deg;) is expected, which is a useful check against the calculated value of &minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"Using S&deg;(CaCO<sub>3</sub>,s) = 92.9, S&deg;(CaO,s) = 39.7 and S&deg;(CO<sub>2</sub>,g) = 214 (all J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;S&deg; for CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g).",choices:["+160.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;160.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+346.6 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+92.9 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;S&deg; = [S&deg;(CaO) + S&deg;(CO<sub>2</sub>)] &minus; S&deg;(CaCO<sub>3</sub>) = (39.7 + 214) &minus; 92.9 = 253.7 &minus; 92.9 = +160.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"Why is &Delta;S&deg; positive for the decomposition CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)?",choices:["One mole of gas is produced from entirely solid reactants, increasing dispersal","Calcium carbonate is more ordered than calcium oxide alone","The reaction is exothermic, and all exothermic reactions have positive &Delta;S&deg;","No moles of gas change on either side of the equation"],explain:"One mole of solid reactant becomes one mole of solid plus one mole of gas; since a gas is far more dispersed than a solid, this is predicted to increase entropy, consistent with the positive calculated value."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does enthalpy change, &Delta;H(system), alone fail to reliably predict whether a reaction will be spontaneous?",choices:["It ignores the entropy change of the surroundings caused by heat transfer, which can also determine spontaneity","Enthalpy change is always exactly zero for a spontaneous reaction","&Delta;H values can never be measured experimentally","Enthalpy change only applies to reactions involving gases"],explain:"&Delta;H(system) ignores the entropy change in the surroundings; many spontaneous reactions are endothermic, so enthalpy alone cannot be the sole criterion for spontaneity."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does the entropy change of the system, &Delta;S(system), alone also fail to reliably predict spontaneity?",choices:["It ignores the (often much larger) entropy change in the surroundings caused by heat transfer","&Delta;S(system) is always negative for a spontaneous reaction","&Delta;S(system) cannot be calculated for any real reaction","Entropy changes of the system are always exactly zero"],explain:"Many spontaneous reactions (such as the Haber process or many precipitations) have a negative &Delta;S(system); the key missing piece is the entropy change of the surroundings, which is often much larger."},
{ch:15,q:"What is the correct criterion, according to the second law of thermodynamics, for a change to be spontaneous?",choices:["&Delta;S(total) = &Delta;S(system) + &Delta;S(surroundings) &gt; 0","&Delta;S(system) &gt; 0, regardless of the surroundings","&Delta;H(system) &lt; 0, regardless of entropy","&Delta;S(surroundings) &gt; 0, regardless of the system"],explain:"The second law of thermodynamics requires the total entropy of system plus surroundings, not the system alone, to increase for a spontaneous change: &Delta;S(total) = &Delta;S(system) + &Delta;S(surroundings) &gt; 0."},
{ch:15,q:"Which equation correctly relates the entropy change of the surroundings to the enthalpy change of the system?",choices:["&Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;&Delta;H(system) &divide; T","&Delta;S(surroundings) = &Delta;H(system) &divide; T","&Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;&Delta;H(system) &times; T","&Delta;S(surroundings) = &Delta;H(system) &times; T"],explain:"&Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;&Delta;H(system) &divide; T (T in kelvin); the negative sign ensures that an exothermic reaction (negative &Delta;H) increases the entropy of the surroundings."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does an exothermic reaction (&Delta;H(system) &lt; 0) increase the entropy of the surroundings?",choices:["Heat is transferred to the surroundings, dispersing energy into them and increasing their entropy","Exothermic reactions always decrease the number of particles in the surroundings","Exothermic reactions never affect the entropy of the surroundings at all","Heat is removed from the surroundings during an exothermic reaction"],explain:"An exothermic reaction releases heat into the surroundings, dispersing energy there and increasing the entropy of the surroundings, which is why &Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;&Delta;H(system)/T gives a positive value when &Delta;H(system) is negative."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does the absolute temperature, T, appear in the denominator of &Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;&Delta;H(system) &divide; T?",choices:["The same quantity of heat causes a much bigger entropy change in cold (low-T) surroundings than in already-hot (high-T) surroundings","Temperature has no real physical effect on entropy, and T is included only to fix the units","A higher temperature always produces a larger entropy change for the same heat transfer","Lower temperatures always decrease the entropy of the surroundings regardless of heat transfer"],explain:"The impact of a given quantity of heat on dispersal depends on how dispersed the surroundings already are: a little extra heat barely changes already-hot, highly dispersed surroundings, but causes a much bigger relative change in cold surroundings, which is why T appears in the denominator."},
{ch:15,q:"For H<sub>2</sub>(g) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(l), using S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>) = 131, S&deg;(O<sub>2</sub>) = 205 and S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>O,l) = 70.0 (J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;S&deg;(system).",choices:["&minus;163.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+163.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;266 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+70.0 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;S&deg; = 70.0 &minus; [131 + (&frac12; &times; 205)] = 70.0 &minus; 233.5 = &minus;163.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, negative as expected since 1.5 mol of gas becomes zero mol of gas."},
{ch:15,q:"For H<sub>2</sub>(g) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(l), &Delta;H&deg; = &minus;286 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Calculate &Delta;S(surroundings) at 298 K.",choices:["+960 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;960 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+0.96 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+286 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Converting &Delta;H to J: &minus;286 000 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. &Delta;S(surroundings) = &minus;(&minus;286 000)/298 = +960 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;S(system) = &minus;163.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S(surroundings) = +960 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, calculate &Delta;S(total) for the formation of liquid water, and state whether the reaction is spontaneous.",choices:["+796 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; spontaneous, since &Delta;S(total) is positive","&minus;796 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; not spontaneous","+1123.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; spontaneous","&minus;163.5 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; not spontaneous"],explain:"&Delta;S(total) = &minus;163.5 + 960 = +796 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Since this is positive, the reaction is spontaneous, even though &Delta;S(system) alone is negative."},
{ch:15,q:"How can an endothermic reaction ever be spontaneous, given that heat flowing into the system from the surroundings decreases &Delta;S(surroundings)?",choices:["If the increase in entropy of the system is large enough to outweigh the decrease in entropy of the surroundings","Endothermic reactions can never actually be spontaneous under any conditions","Because &Delta;S(surroundings) is always positive for an endothermic reaction","Because entropy of the system is irrelevant to spontaneity"],explain:"An endothermic reaction can be spontaneous only if the increase in &Delta;S(system) is large enough to more than compensate for the decrease in &Delta;S(surroundings) caused by heat flowing from the surroundings into the system."},
{ch:15,q:"Starting from &Delta;S(total) = &Delta;S(system) &minus; &Delta;H(system)/T &gt; 0 and multiplying through by &minus;T, which function is obtained?",choices:["&Delta;G(system) = &Delta;H(system) &minus; T&Delta;S(system)","&Delta;G(system) = &Delta;H(system) + T&Delta;S(system)","&Delta;G(system) = T&Delta;H(system) &minus; &Delta;S(system)","&Delta;G(system) = &minus;&Delta;H(system) &minus; T&Delta;S(system)"],explain:"Multiplying &Delta;S(total) = &Delta;S(system) &minus; &Delta;H(system)/T by &minus;T (and reversing the inequality, since T is always positive) gives &Delta;G(system) = &Delta;H(system) &minus; T&Delta;S(system) = &minus;T&Delta;S(total)."},
{ch:15,q:"Since &Delta;G(system) = &minus;T&Delta;S(total), and &Delta;S(total) must be positive for a spontaneous change, what must be true of &Delta;G(system) for a spontaneous change?",choices:["&Delta;G(system) must be negative","&Delta;G(system) must be positive","&Delta;G(system) must be exactly zero","&Delta;G(system) can be either sign for a spontaneous change"],explain:"Because T is always positive, &Delta;G(system) = &minus;T&Delta;S(total) is negative exactly when &Delta;S(total) is positive, so &Delta;G(system) must be negative for a spontaneous change."},
{ch:15,q:"Why is Gibbs free energy, &Delta;G, generally more useful in practice than calculating &Delta;S(surroundings) and &Delta;S(total) separately every time?",choices:["&Delta;G combines enthalpy and entropy of the system alone into one quantity that correctly predicts spontaneity, without a separate surroundings calculation","&Delta;G is always numerically equal to &Delta;S(system)","&Delta;G can only be used for reactions involving gases","&Delta;G removes the need to consider temperature at all"],explain:"&Delta;G(system) = &Delta;H(system) &minus; T&Delta;S(system) already incorporates the entropy change of the surroundings implicitly, so it predicts spontaneity directly from data about the system, without a separate surroundings calculation each time."},
{ch:15,q:"What does a negative value of &Delta;G&deg; indicate about a reaction under standard conditions?",choices:["The reaction is spontaneous (thermodynamically favourable) as written","The reaction is non-spontaneous as written","The system is at equilibrium","The reaction has no defined enthalpy change"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0 means the reaction is spontaneous as written under standard conditions."},
{ch:15,q:"What does a positive value of &Delta;G&deg; indicate about a reaction under standard conditions?",choices:["The reaction as written is non-spontaneous, but the reverse reaction is spontaneous","The reaction is spontaneous as written","The system is at equilibrium","The reaction releases a large amount of heat"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0 means the forward reaction is non-spontaneous as written under standard conditions; since reversing a reaction reverses the sign of &Delta;G&deg;, the reverse reaction is spontaneous."},
{ch:15,q:"What does &Delta;G&deg; = 0 indicate about a reaction?",choices:["The system is at equilibrium","The reaction proceeds completely to products","The reaction is impossible under any conditions","The enthalpy change is exactly zero"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = 0 means the system is at equilibrium; there is no net thermodynamic tendency to proceed in either direction."},
{ch:15,q:"Does a negative &Delta;G&deg; guarantee that a reaction will occur quickly?",choices:["No; &Delta;G&deg; is a thermodynamic criterion and gives no information about reaction rate (kinetics)","Yes; a negative &Delta;G&deg; always means the reaction is instantaneous","Yes, but only for reactions involving gases","No; a negative &Delta;G&deg; actually means the reaction cannot occur at all"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; only predicts whether a reaction is thermodynamically favourable, not how fast it will occur; a spontaneous reaction with a high activation energy may need heating or a catalyst before it proceeds at a noticeable rate."},
{ch:15,q:"Which equation allows &Delta;G&deg; to be calculated at 298 K directly from tabulated data, analogous to calculating &Delta;H&deg; from &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg; values?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; = &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(reactants)","&Delta;G&deg; = &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(reactants) &minus; &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products)","&Delta;G&deg; = &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products) + &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(reactants)","&Delta;G&deg; = &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products) &minus; S&deg;(reactants)"],explain:"Just as for &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;, &Delta;G&deg; = &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(reactants), and &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; of an element in its standard state is zero by definition."},
{ch:15,q:"Why can &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; values only be used to calculate &Delta;G&deg; at 298 K, not at other temperatures?",choices:["Tabulated &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; values are specific to standard conditions (298 K); &Delta;G at other temperatures requires &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; combined with &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S","&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; values change too unpredictably with temperature to ever be useful","&Delta;G&deg; is only defined at 298 K and has no meaning at any other temperature","&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; values can be used at any temperature without modification"],explain:"Since tabulated &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; values apply specifically to 298 K, calculating &Delta;G at a different temperature requires &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; (which barely change with temperature) applied directly in &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;H&deg; = +178 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +160.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> for CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), calculate &Delta;G&deg; at 298 K.",choices:["+130 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;130 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+178 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+47.9 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"Converting &Delta;S&deg; to kJ: 0.1608 kJ K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. &Delta;G&deg; = 178 &minus; (298 &times; 0.1608) = 178 &minus; 47.9 = +130 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"What does the positive value of &Delta;G&deg; (+130 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) for the decomposition of calcium carbonate at 298 K tell us?",choices:["The decomposition is non-spontaneous at 298 K, so calcium carbonate is stable at room temperature","The decomposition is spontaneous and occurs rapidly at 298 K","Calcium carbonate cannot exist as a stable solid under any conditions","The reaction releases 130 kJ of heat per mole at 298 K"],explain:"A positive &Delta;G&deg; at 298 K means the decomposition is non-spontaneous as written at room temperature, consistent with the everyday stability of limestone (CaCO<sub>3</sub>)."},
{ch:15,q:"For a reaction with &Delta;H&deg; negative and &Delta;S&deg; positive, how does spontaneity depend on temperature?",choices:["The reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures, since both terms in &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S are favourable","The reaction is never spontaneous at any temperature","The reaction is spontaneous only at low temperature","The reaction is spontaneous only at high temperature"],explain:"If &Delta;H&deg; is negative and &Delta;S&deg; is positive, both &Delta;H (negative) and &minus;T&Delta;S (also negative, since T is always positive) favour a negative &Delta;G at every temperature, so the reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures."},
{ch:15,q:"For a reaction with &Delta;H&deg; positive and &Delta;S&deg; negative, how does spontaneity depend on temperature?",choices:["The reaction is never spontaneous at any temperature","The reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures","The reaction is spontaneous only at low temperature","The reaction is spontaneous only at high temperature"],explain:"If &Delta;H&deg; is positive and &Delta;S&deg; is negative, both &Delta;H (positive) and &minus;T&Delta;S (also positive) make &Delta;G positive at every temperature, so the reaction is never spontaneous."},
{ch:15,q:"For a reaction with &Delta;H&deg; negative and &Delta;S&deg; negative, how does spontaneity depend on temperature?",choices:["Spontaneous only at low temperature","Spontaneous only at high temperature","Spontaneous at all temperatures","Never spontaneous at any temperature"],explain:"When &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; are both negative, &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S is negative at low T (where the small, positive &minus;T&Delta;S term does not overcome the negative &Delta;H), but becomes positive once T is large enough that &minus;T&Delta;S dominates, so the reaction is spontaneous only at low temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"For a reaction with &Delta;H&deg; positive and &Delta;S&deg; positive, how does spontaneity depend on temperature?",choices:["Spontaneous only at high temperature","Spontaneous only at low temperature","Spontaneous at all temperatures","Never spontaneous at any temperature"],explain:"When &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; are both positive, &Delta;G is positive at low T (where &minus;T&Delta;S is small) but becomes negative once T is large enough that the negative &minus;T&Delta;S term dominates the positive &Delta;H, so the reaction is spontaneous only at high temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"Which equation gives the crossover temperature, T<sub>spontaneous</sub>, at which &Delta;G&deg; = 0?",choices:["T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &Delta;H&deg; &divide; &Delta;S&deg;","T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &Delta;S&deg; &divide; &Delta;H&deg;","T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &Delta;H&deg; &times; &Delta;S&deg;","T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &Delta;G&deg; &divide; &Delta;S&deg;"],explain:"Setting &Delta;G&deg; = &Delta;H&deg; &minus; T&Delta;S&deg; = 0 and rearranging gives T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &Delta;H&deg; &divide; &Delta;S&deg;."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;H&deg; = +178 000 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +160.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> for the decomposition of CaCO<sub>3</sub>, calculate the crossover temperature above which decomposition becomes spontaneous.",choices:["1107 K","710 K","298 K","834 K"],explain:"T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = 178 000 &divide; 160.8 = 1107 K (about 834 &deg;C)."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does the crossover temperature for the decomposition of calcium carbonate (1107 K, about 834 &deg;C) match industrial practice?",choices:["Lime kilns are operated at around 1000&ndash;1100 &deg;C to thermally decompose limestone into quicklime","Limestone is always stored below 0 &deg;C to prevent any decomposition","Calcium carbonate decomposes spontaneously even at room temperature in industrial kilns","The crossover temperature has no relevance to how lime kilns are actually operated"],explain:"Since &Delta;G&deg; only becomes negative above about 1107 K (834 &deg;C), industrial lime kilns are operated at around 1000&ndash;1100 &deg;C specifically to make the decomposition of limestone to quicklime thermodynamically favourable."},
{ch:15,q:"For the Haber process, &Delta;H&deg; = &minus;92 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = &minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Calculate the crossover temperature.",choices:["465 K","92 K","198 K","910 K"],explain:"T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &minus;92 000 &divide; (&minus;198) = 465 K."},
{ch:15,q:"For the Haber process (&Delta;H&deg; = &minus;92 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, &Delta;S&deg; = &minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;G&deg; at 298 K.",choices:["&minus;33.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+33.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;92 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;59.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;92 &minus; [298 &times; (&minus;0.198)] = &minus;92 + 59.0 = &minus;33.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, negative (spontaneous), consistent with 298 K being below the 465 K crossover temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"For the Haber process (&Delta;H&deg; = &minus;92 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, &Delta;S&deg; = &minus;198 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;G&deg; at 800 K.",choices:["+66.4 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;66.4 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+158.4 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;92 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;92 &minus; [800 &times; (&minus;0.198)] = &minus;92 + 158.4 = +66.4 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, positive (non-spontaneous), consistent with 800 K being above the 465 K crossover temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"Given that the Haber process is only thermodynamically spontaneous below about 465 K, why do industrial ammonia plants actually operate at around 670&ndash;720 K (400&ndash;450 &deg;C)?",choices:["This is a practical compromise: a lower, more thermodynamically favourable temperature would make the reaction rate impractically slow, so a catalyst and moderate heating are used instead","The crossover temperature calculation must be wrong, since industrial plants never operate below the crossover temperature","Operating above the crossover temperature actually increases the equilibrium yield of ammonia","Industrial plants do not use a catalyst, so higher temperature is required to achieve any reaction at all"],explain:"Although lower temperature favours a higher equilibrium yield of ammonia, the reaction would be far too slow; industrial plants use an iron catalyst and a moderate temperature as a practical balance between reaction rate and equilibrium yield."},
{ch:15,q:"Which of the following best describes the role of temperature in &Delta;G&deg; = &Delta;H&deg; &minus; T&Delta;S&deg;, when &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; have the same sign?",choices:["Temperature acts like a 'tap' controlling how much the &minus;T&Delta;S&deg; term contributes relative to the fixed &Delta;H&deg; term, which is why spontaneity can switch over as T changes","Temperature has no effect on &Delta;G&deg; once &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; are known","&Delta;G&deg; is always equal to &Delta;H&deg;, regardless of temperature, when the signs match","Temperature only matters when &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; have opposite signs"],explain:"At low T, &Delta;G&deg; is dominated by the fixed &Delta;H&deg; term; as T increases, the &minus;T&Delta;S&deg; term grows and eventually dominates, so temperature controls the balance between the two contributions, especially when their signs match and a crossover exists."},
{ch:15,q:"Which equation correctly relates &Delta;G&deg; to the equilibrium constant, K?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = RT ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT &divide; ln K","&Delta;G&deg; = R &divide; (T ln K)"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, where R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and T is in kelvin, links the standard Gibbs energy change directly to the equilibrium constant."},
{ch:15,q:"In the equation &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, in what unit must &Delta;G&deg; be expressed for R = 8.31 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> to give a consistent result?",choices:["J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, not kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, not J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","It does not matter, since K has no units"],explain:"Since R is given in J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, &Delta;G&deg; must be converted from the usual kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> into J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> before use, or the calculated K (or &Delta;G&deg;) will be wrong by a factor of 1000."},
{ch:15,q:"According to &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, what does a large, negative value of &Delta;G&deg; correspond to?",choices:["A very large value of K (K &gt;&gt; 1), with the equilibrium lying strongly towards products","A very small value of K (K &lt;&lt; 1), with the equilibrium lying strongly towards reactants","K must equal exactly zero","K cannot be determined from &Delta;G&deg; alone"],explain:"A large negative &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to a large positive value of ln K, and hence a very large K, meaning the equilibrium lies strongly towards products."},
{ch:15,q:"According to &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, what value of K corresponds to &Delta;G&deg; = 0?",choices:["K = 1","K = 0","K is undefined","K = infinity"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = 0 corresponds to ln K = 0, so K = 1: an equilibrium mixture with comparable, appreciable amounts of both reactants and products."},
{ch:15,q:"According to &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, what does a large, positive value of &Delta;G&deg; correspond to?",choices:["A very small value of K (K &lt;&lt; 1), with the equilibrium lying strongly towards reactants","A very large value of K (K &gt;&gt; 1)","K = 1 exactly","The reaction cannot reach equilibrium at all"],explain:"A large positive &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to a large negative value of ln K, and hence a very small K, meaning the equilibrium lies strongly towards reactants."},
{ch:15,q:"The esterification reaction CH<sub>3</sub>COOH(aq) + C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH(aq) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(aq) has &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;4.38 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 298 K. Calculate ln K.",choices:["1.77","4.38","0.177","17.7"],explain:"ln K = &minus;&Delta;G&deg; &divide; RT = &minus;(&minus;4380 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) &divide; (8.31 &times; 298) = 4380 &divide; 2476.4 = 1.77."},
{ch:15,q:"Using ln K = 1.77 for the esterification reaction above, calculate the equilibrium constant, K.",choices:["5.9","1.77","0.177","177"],explain:"K = e<sup>1.77</sup> &asymp; 5.9. Since K &gt; 1, the equilibrium mixture contains somewhat more ester and water than unreacted acid and alcohol, but appreciable amounts of all four species remain present."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does the esterification reaction above (K &asymp; 5.9) not go to completion, even though &Delta;G&deg; is negative?",choices:["A moderate K value (not vastly greater than 1) corresponds to an equilibrium mixture containing appreciable amounts of both reactants and products","Any negative &Delta;G&deg; value, no matter how small, always drives a reaction fully to completion","The reaction is actually at equilibrium with K = 0, meaning no product forms","&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K does not apply to esterification reactions"],explain:"A K value around 5.9, while greater than 1, is not vastly larger than 1, so the equilibrium mixture contains appreciable amounts of both reactants and products, consistent with esterification being a classic reversible, incomplete reaction."},
{ch:15,q:"At 298 K, the equilibrium constant for 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) is K = 1.7 &times; 10<sup>12</sup>. Calculate ln K.",choices:["28.16","1.7","12","41.4"],explain:"ln(1.7 &times; 10<sup>12</sup>) = ln(1.7) + 12 ln(10) = 0.531 + 27.63 = 28.16."},
{ch:15,q:"Using ln K = 28.16 at 298 K, calculate &Delta;G&deg; for 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g).",choices:["&minus;69.7 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+69.7 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;233.8 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;28.16 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K = &minus;(8.31 &times; 298) &times; 28.16 = &minus;2476.4 &times; 28.16 = &minus;69 739 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = &minus;69.7 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"What does the large, negative &Delta;G&deg; (&minus;69.7 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) for 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) correctly predict about this reaction's position of equilibrium?",choices:["The equilibrium lies overwhelmingly towards products, consistent with the huge value of K = 1.7 &times; 10<sup>12</sup>","The equilibrium lies overwhelmingly towards reactants","The reaction cannot actually reach equilibrium","The reaction must be endothermic, since &Delta;G&deg; is negative"],explain:"A large negative &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to a very large K, confirming the equilibrium lies overwhelmingly towards the NO<sub>2</sub> product &ndash; consistent with how efficiently NO emitted from engines is converted to NO<sub>2</sub> in the atmosphere."},
{ch:15,q:"Which relationship correctly links the magnitude of &Delta;G&deg; to how far a reaction proceeds towards products at equilibrium?",choices:["The more negative &Delta;G&deg; is, the larger K is, and the further the reaction proceeds towards products","The more positive &Delta;G&deg; is, the larger K is","&Delta;G&deg; has no relationship to the extent of reaction at equilibrium","The magnitude of &Delta;G&deg; only matters if the reaction involves gases"],explain:"Since &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, a more negative &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to a larger K and hence a reaction that proceeds further towards products at equilibrium; this is the direct link between thermodynamics and the position of equilibrium."},
{ch:15,q:"For a hypothetical reaction with &Delta;H = 0 and &Delta;S = 0 (e.g. an ideal gas reactant converting to an ideal gas product with no bonds broken or formed), what is &Delta;G for this reaction?",choices:["Zero","Strongly positive","Strongly negative","Cannot be determined"],explain:"Since &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S, if both &Delta;H and &Delta;S are zero, then &Delta;G = 0 for this idealized reaction."},
{ch:15,q:"For the hypothetical reaction reactant(g) &rarr; product(g) with &Delta;G&deg; = 0, why does the reaction still proceed partway towards an equilibrium mixture, rather than remaining as pure reactant?",choices:["A mixture of reactant and product has higher total entropy than either pure reactant or pure product alone, and the total entropy is maximized at the equilibrium composition","The reaction has no thermodynamic driving force at all, so nothing happens","&Delta;G&deg; = 0 means the reaction is strongly non-spontaneous","Pure reactant always has higher entropy than any mixture"],explain:"Even with &Delta;H = &Delta;S = 0 for the overall conversion, a mixture of reactant and product has greater entropy (more ways to arrange the two types of particle together) than either pure substance, so the system moves towards the mixture that maximizes total entropy, which is the equilibrium state."},
{ch:15,q:"At equilibrium, how does the total Gibbs energy of the reaction mixture compare with its value at any other composition along the reaction pathway?",choices:["The total Gibbs energy is at a minimum at equilibrium","The total Gibbs energy is at a maximum at equilibrium","The total Gibbs energy is undefined at equilibrium","The total Gibbs energy is always zero at equilibrium"],explain:"As a reaction proceeds (from either pure reactants or pure products), the total Gibbs energy of the mixture decreases until it reaches a minimum value at the equilibrium composition; any further change would increase Gibbs energy and is therefore non-spontaneous."},
{ch:15,q:"If &Delta;G&deg; for a reaction is negative, how does the equilibrium mixture's composition compare with the starting pure reactants?",choices:["The equilibrium mixture contains mainly products (K &gt; 1)","The equilibrium mixture contains mainly unreacted reactants (K &lt; 1)","The equilibrium mixture contains exactly equal amounts of reactants and products","No equilibrium mixture can form if &Delta;G&deg; is negative"],explain:"A negative &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to K &gt; 1, so the Gibbs energy of the pure products is lower than that of the pure reactants, and the equilibrium mixture consists mainly of products."},
{ch:15,q:"If &Delta;G&deg; for a reaction is positive, how does the equilibrium mixture's composition compare with the starting pure reactants?",choices:["The equilibrium mixture contains mainly unreacted reactants (K &lt; 1)","The equilibrium mixture contains mainly products (K &gt; 1)","The reaction cannot reach equilibrium at all","The equilibrium mixture contains exactly equal amounts of reactants and products"],explain:"A positive &Delta;G&deg; corresponds to K &lt; 1, so the Gibbs energy of the pure reactants is lower than that of the pure products, and the equilibrium mixture consists mainly of unreacted reactants."},
{ch:15,q:"Water boils according to H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g). Using S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>O,l) = 70.0 and S&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>O,g) = 188.8 (J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;S&deg; for this process.",choices:["+118.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;118.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+258.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+70.0 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;S&deg; = S&deg;(gas) &minus; S&deg;(liquid) = 188.8 &minus; 70.0 = +118.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, positive as expected, since a liquid converts to a much more dispersed gas."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>O,l) = &minus;286 and &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;(H<sub>2</sub>O,g) = &minus;242 (kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>), calculate &Delta;H&deg; for H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O(g).",choices:["+44 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;44 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+528 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;286 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;H&deg; = &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;(gas) &minus; &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;(liquid) = (&minus;242) &minus; (&minus;286) = +44 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, positive, as expected for the breaking of intermolecular hydrogen bonds on vaporization."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;H&deg; = +44 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +118.8 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> for the vaporization of water, estimate the boiling point of water from the crossover temperature T = &Delta;H&deg;/&Delta;S&deg;.",choices:["370 K (about 97 &deg;C)","118.8 K","44 K","298 K"],explain:"T = 44 000 &divide; 118.8 = 370 K (about 97 &deg;C), close to the known boiling point of 373 K (100 &deg;C); the small discrepancy arises because &Delta;H and &Delta;S are assumed constant with temperature, which is only an approximation."},
{ch:15,q:"Why does the crossover-temperature calculation for the boiling point of water (370 K) not give exactly the correct value of 373 K?",choices:["The calculation assumes &Delta;H and &Delta;S do not change with temperature, which is only an approximation","The entropy values used were measured in the wrong units","Water does not actually have a well-defined boiling point","The equation &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S does not apply to phase changes"],explain:"The method assumes that &Delta;H and &Delta;S remain constant between 298 K and the boiling point, which is only an approximation; in reality both vary somewhat with temperature, producing a small discrepancy from the true boiling point."},
{ch:15,q:"A reaction has &Delta;H&deg; = +10.00 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +10.00 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> at 298 K. What is &Delta;G&deg; for the reaction?",choices:["+7.02 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+10.00 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;2.98 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+2.98 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = 10.00 &minus; (298 &times; 10.00/1000) = 10.00 &minus; 2.98 = +7.02 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"A reaction has &Delta;H&deg; = +100 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +100 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Calculate the crossover temperature, and state what this tells you about the reaction at 500 K.",choices:["T = 1000 K; at 500 K (below the crossover) the reaction is non-spontaneous","T = 1000 K; at 500 K the reaction is spontaneous","T = 100 K; at 500 K the reaction is non-spontaneous","T = 10 K; at 500 K the reaction is spontaneous"],explain:"T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = 100 000 &divide; 100 = 1000 K. Since &Delta;H and &Delta;S are both positive, the reaction is spontaneous only above 1000 K; at 500 K, which is below the crossover, the reaction is non-spontaneous."},
{ch:15,q:"Ammonium chloride dissolves in water spontaneously in an endothermic process, NH<sub>4</sub>Cl(s) &rarr; NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq). What best explains this observation?",choices:["The entropy increase of the system (solid dissolving into dispersed aqueous ions) is large enough to make &Delta;G negative despite the endothermic &Delta;H","Endothermic processes are always energetically favourable","The bonds within solid NH<sub>4</sub>Cl must be extremely weak","The process cannot actually be spontaneous, since it is endothermic"],explain:"Dissolving a solid into aqueous ions strongly increases entropy (dispersal), and this entropy increase of the system is large enough to make &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S negative, overcoming the unfavourable (endothermic) enthalpy change."},
{ch:15,q:"A reaction has &Delta;H&deg; = +100 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +100 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. At what temperature does the reaction become spontaneous?",choices:["Above 1000 K","Below 1000 K","Above 100 K","The reaction is never spontaneous, regardless of temperature"],explain:"Since &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; are both positive, the reaction is spontaneous only above the crossover temperature, T = 100 000/100 = 1000 K."},
{ch:15,q:"For the reaction A(s) + B(aq) &rarr; C(aq) + D(g), &Delta;H&deg; = +100 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = +100 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Which statement is correct?",choices:["The reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures above 1000 K","The reaction is not spontaneous at any temperature","The reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures","The reaction is spontaneous at all temperatures below 1000 K"],explain:"With &Delta;H&deg; positive and &Delta;S&deg; positive, this reaction follows the 'spontaneous only at high T' pattern, with a crossover temperature of 100 000/100 = 1000 K; it is spontaneous only above this temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"What is the standard entropy and standard Gibbs energy of formation, &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;, of any element in its standard (most stable) state?",choices:["S&deg; is positive (never zero); &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; is zero by definition","Both S&deg; and &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; are zero for every element","S&deg; is zero; &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; is positive","Both S&deg; and &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; are positive for every element"],explain:"&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg; of an element in its standard state is zero by definition, exactly like &Delta;H<sub>f</sub>&deg;; but absolute entropy, S&deg;, is never zero for a real substance above 0 K, including elements."},
{ch:15,q:"Using &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>,s) = &minus;742 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>,s) = &minus;1582 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, calculate &Delta;G&deg; for 2Al(s) + Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; 2Fe(s) + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s).",choices:["&minus;840 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+840 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;2324 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;1582 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(products) &minus; &Sigma;&Delta;G<sub>f</sub>&deg;(reactants) = (&minus;1582) &minus; (&minus;742) = &minus;840 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> (Al and Fe, as elements, contribute zero)."},
{ch:15,q:"What does the large, negative &Delta;G&deg; (&minus;840 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) for the thermite reaction, 2Al(s) + Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; 2Fe(s) + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s), indicate?",choices:["The reaction is strongly spontaneous under standard conditions","The reaction is strongly non-spontaneous under standard conditions","The reaction has a very large, positive entropy change","The reaction absorbs a large amount of heat"],explain:"A large negative &Delta;G&deg; indicates the reaction is strongly thermodynamically favourable (spontaneous) under standard conditions, consistent with the vigorous, highly exothermic thermite reaction."},
{ch:15,q:"Which pair of properties correctly describes a system at equilibrium?",choices:["Maximum total entropy; minimum total Gibbs energy","Minimum total entropy; minimum total Gibbs energy","Maximum total entropy; maximum total Gibbs energy","Minimum total entropy; maximum total Gibbs energy"],explain:"At equilibrium, the total entropy of the universe (system plus surroundings) is at a maximum, which is equivalent to the total Gibbs energy of the system being at a minimum."},
{ch:15,q:"Which combination of &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; values means a reaction is essentially a complete reaction (strongly favoured) at every temperature?",choices:["&Delta;H&deg; negative, &Delta;S&deg; positive","&Delta;H&deg; positive, &Delta;S&deg; negative","&Delta;H&deg; positive, &Delta;S&deg; positive","&Delta;H&deg; negative, &Delta;S&deg; negative"],explain:"When &Delta;H&deg; is negative and &Delta;S&deg; is positive, both terms in &Delta;G = &Delta;H &minus; T&Delta;S favour a negative &Delta;G at every temperature, making the reaction spontaneous (and typically going essentially to completion) at all temperatures."},
{ch:15,q:"Which combination of &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; signs describes a reaction whose spontaneity can be reversed simply by changing the temperature?",choices:["&Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; having the same sign (both positive, or both negative)","&Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; always having opposite signs","&Delta;H&deg; = 0 and &Delta;S&deg; = 0","&Delta;S&deg; = 0, regardless of the sign of &Delta;H&deg;"],explain:"Only when &Delta;H&deg; and &Delta;S&deg; have the same sign (both positive, giving a high-T crossover, or both negative, giving a low-T crossover) does a finite crossover temperature exist, allowing spontaneity to switch over as temperature is changed. When the signs are opposite, the reaction is either always or never spontaneous, with no crossover."},
{ch:15,q:"Magnesium carbonate decomposes at high temperature: MgCO<sub>3</sub>(s) &rarr; MgO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g). At a temperature well above the point at which this becomes spontaneous, what are the expected signs of &Delta;G, &Delta;H and &Delta;S?",choices:["&Delta;G negative, &Delta;H positive, &Delta;S positive","&Delta;G positive, &Delta;H positive, &Delta;S negative","&Delta;G negative, &Delta;H negative, &Delta;S positive","&Delta;G positive, &Delta;H negative, &Delta;S negative"],explain:"Like CaCO<sub>3</sub>, this decomposition is endothermic (&Delta;H positive, bonds must be broken) with a positive entropy change (one mole of gas is produced from solid reactants); well above the crossover temperature, &minus;T&Delta;S dominates and &Delta;G becomes negative, so the reaction is spontaneous."},
{ch:15,q:"Propene reacts with hydrogen to form propane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>6</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>(g), with &Delta;H&deg; = &minus;123 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> and &Delta;S&deg; = &minus;128 J K<sup>&minus;1</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. Calculate the crossover temperature above which this hydrogenation ceases to be spontaneous.",choices:["961 K","123 K","128 K","1.04 K"],explain:"T<sub>spontaneous</sub> = &minus;123 000 &divide; (&minus;128) = 961 K; below 961 K the reaction (both &Delta;H and &Delta;S negative) is spontaneous, and above 961 K it is not."},
{ch:15,q:"Iodine reacts with hydrogen to form hydrogen iodide, H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HI(g), with K = 160 at 500 K. Calculate ln K.",choices:["5.08","160","2.20","0.80"],explain:"ln(160) = 5.08."},
{ch:15,q:"Using ln K = 5.08 at 500 K, calculate &Delta;G&deg; for H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HI(g).",choices:["&minus;21.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+21.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;2538 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;5.08 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K = &minus;(8.31 &times; 500) &times; 5.08 = &minus;4155 &times; 5.08 = &minus;21 110 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = &minus;21.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:15,q:"What is the value of &Delta;G&deg; for a reaction in which the equilibrium constant K = 1, at any temperature?",choices:["Zero","Infinite","Equal to &minus;RT","Undefined"],explain:"Since &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K and ln(1) = 0, &Delta;G&deg; = 0 whenever K = 1, regardless of the temperature."},
{ch:15,q:"A reaction has &Delta;G&deg; greater than about +30 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What can be said about the extent of this reaction?",choices:["Spontaneous change is essentially impossible; almost no reaction occurs, and K is very small","The reaction goes essentially to completion","The reaction reaches an equilibrium with K = 1","Nothing can be concluded about the extent of reaction from &Delta;G&deg; alone"],explain:"A strongly positive &Delta;G&deg; (greater than about +30 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) corresponds, via &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K, to an extremely small K, so essentially no forward reaction occurs."},
{ch:15,q:"A reaction has &Delta;G&deg; more negative than about &minus;30 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>. What can be said about the extent of this reaction?",choices:["The reaction goes essentially to completion, with K &gt;&gt; 1","Spontaneous change is essentially impossible","The reaction reaches an equilibrium with K = 1","The reaction is endothermic"],explain:"A strongly negative &Delta;G&deg; (more negative than about &minus;30 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) corresponds to an extremely large K, so the reaction proceeds essentially to completion."},
{ch:15,q:"Which of the following distinguishes entropy, S, from Gibbs free energy, G, as a criterion for spontaneity?",choices:["Spontaneity requires the entropy of the universe (system + surroundings) to increase, whereas &Delta;G(system) &lt; 0 alone is sufficient and only requires data about the system","Entropy and Gibbs energy are simply two different names for exactly the same quantity","Gibbs energy can only be used for reactions with no enthalpy change","Entropy of the system alone is always a sufficient criterion for spontaneity, making Gibbs energy unnecessary"],explain:"The fundamental criterion for spontaneity is an increase in the total entropy of system plus surroundings; Gibbs free energy change of the system alone, &Delta;G(system) &lt; 0, is mathematically equivalent to this but only requires data about the system, making it far more convenient to use."},
{ch:15,q:"Which statement correctly summarizes the relationship between &Delta;G and the quantity of useful work obtainable from a spontaneous reaction?",choices:["The magnitude of &Delta;G represents the maximum energy available to do useful work, rather than simply being lost as heat to the surroundings","&Delta;G represents the total heat released by a reaction, with no relation to useful work","&Delta;G is always exactly equal in magnitude to &Delta;H for any spontaneous reaction","&Delta;G has no connection to the amount of useful work obtainable from a reaction"],explain:"Whereas &Delta;H measures the total quantity of heat exchanged, &Delta;G measures the quality of that energy: specifically the maximum amount that is free to do useful work (such as generating electricity in a fuel cell), rather than simply being dispersed as heat into the surroundings."}
]
